    {
      "id": "shrinks",
      "order": 2,
      "title": "The construct form often shrinks",
      "kind": "single",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Leaning on the next word for meaning often costs the construct noun some of its own stress, and an unstressed long vowel tends to shorten. That's why “word” is דָּבָר (davar) on its own but דְּבַר (devar) in “word of the LORD”.",
        "It's a tendency, not an iron rule -- short, one-syllable nouns often show no visible change at all. The pointing always tells you which case you're looking at; there's no need to guess."
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "ref": "Jonah.1.1",
          "surface_form": "דְּבַר",
          "transliteration": "devar",
          "prefix_form": "",
          "prefix_morphemes": [],
          "base_form": "דְּבַר",
          "suffix_form": "",
          "suffix_kind": null,
          "suffix_pgn": null,
          "lemma_citation_form": "דָּבָר",
          "lemma_transliteration": "davar",
          "gloss": "word, thing",
          "highlight": "base",
          "note": "Same word as the opening-line example above, looked at differently: the construct form's vowel is visibly shorter than the dictionary form."
        },
        {
          "ref": "Jonah.2.5",
          "surface_form": "הֵיכַל",
